al differential and constitutional symptoms suggest neoplastic rather than reactive process", "Stress-Induced Leukocytosis: Anxiety history insufficient to explain 66k/mm³ with systemic symptoms"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Fatigue with constitutional symptoms", "Marked leukocytosis (66k/mm³)", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4433054348333905, "posterior": 0.4433054348333905, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.023, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.36924229923168606, "posterior": 0.36924229923168606, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.11772726141236645, "posterior": 0.11772726141236645, "danger": 0.65, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.047, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of constitutional symptoms (fatigue, weight loss) and elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAKP) support lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders (B4) over other options?", "summary": "Elevated LAKP contradicts lymphoid neoplasms (B4) but is non-specific for plasma cell disorders. Constitutional symptoms slightly favor B4 over reactive causes but are outweighed by LAKP findings."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAKP) in a 59-year-old with recent infection (cold) and normal differential support a leukemoid reaction (B5) over chronic MPNs (B3)?", "summary": "Elevated LAKP argues against chronic MPN (B3) but is atypical for leukemoid reaction. Recent infection weakens B5 but cannot fully explain constitutional symptoms and LAKP elevation."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal peripheral blood differential with marked leukocytosis (66,500/mm³) and elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAKP) argue against myeloid neoplasm with blasts (e.g., AML) in favor …", "summary": "Normal differential and LAKP strongly argue against myeloid blasts (B1), as AML typically presents with abnormal cell morphology and low LAKP. This favors chronic MPN (B3) over AML."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAKP) in a subacute presentation with normal differential argue against CML in chronic/accelerated phase (B3 representative) in favor of other MPNs o…", "summary": "Elevated LAKP directly contradicts CML in chronic phase, which typically has low LAKP. This supports other MPNs with higher LAKP (e.g., PV/ET) and weakens B3 as a whole."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal peripheral blood differential and absence of lymphadenopathy/splenomegaly on physical exam argue against lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders (B4) in favor of non-lymphoid causes?", "summary": "Normal differential and lack of lymphadenopathy strongly argue against lymphoid neoplasms (B4), as B-CLL or lymphomas typically show lymphocytosis or palpable lymphadenopathy. This favors myeloid or non-lymphoid processes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute course with weight loss and abdominal pain in a patient with 66k leukocytosis argue against reactive leukemoid reaction (B5) in favor of neoplastic causes?", "summary": "Subacute constitutional symptoms and persistent leukocytosis without clear reactive triggers (infection, inflammation) strongly argue against B5. This supports neoplastic causes like B3 or B1."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.413], ["B3", 0.344], ["B1.1", 0.275]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch B1.1 (AML) has posterior 0.275 (below 0.75 threshold). Multiple dangerous alternatives remain unresolved (B1.1: 0.275 posterior, B3.4: 0.034 posterior but 0.8 danger). No info exhaustion as key discriminators like peripheral blood smear morphology or bone marrow biopsy remain unasked."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2751911049339819, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Leukocytosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.413}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "posterior": 0.443, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=primekg]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
